Detect a command that fails to start under xargs -P on Windows.
The handle returned by mingw_spawn_proc was tested with "p < 0", which
is never true for a pointer, so a failure was stored as a process
handle. -1 is the current process pseudo-handle, and the final wait
for the remaining commands waited for xargs itself, forever:
"xargs -P2 no_such_command" hung instead of failing with status 127.

Compare the result with -1 before converting it, report the error, and
give the reserved slot back so it isn't waited on.

---
 findutils/xargs.c |   10 ++++++----
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

--- a/findutils/xargs.c
+++ b/findutils/xargs.c
@@ -342,11 +342,13 @@
 		int idx;
 		status = !G.running_procs && !G.max_procs ? 0 : wait_for_slot(&idx);
 		if (G.max_procs) {
-			HANDLE p = (HANDLE)mingw_spawn_proc((const char **)G.args);
-			if (p < 0)
+			intptr_t p = mingw_spawn_proc((const char **)G.args);
+			if (p == -1) {
+				/* Give back the slot */
+				G.running_procs--;
 				status = -1;
-			else
-				G.procs[idx] = p;
+			} else
+				G.procs[idx] = (HANDLE)p;
 		} else {
 			while (G.running_procs) {
 				int status2 = wait_for_slot(&idx);
